Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period gothic live-action.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A shuttered bedroom lies in pitch darkness. THE OLD MAN, thin and elderly with a white beard and a pale blue eye filmed over, sleeps beneath a heavy bed. NARRATOR, gaunt and close-haired in a black waistcoat, watches from the darkness with a closed lantern.

(SOUND: wind presses against the shutters; the old house settles with faint creaks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

The OLD MAN turns in his sleep. His eyelid lifts. The pale blue, filmed eye catches a sliver of moonlight. NARRATOR recoils.

(SOUND: one floorboard gives a small, dry creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

The eye closes. NARRATOR's hand tightens around the lantern.

(SOUND: NARRATOR's breathing trembles, then stops)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

NARRATOR leans toward the sleeping face, unable to look anywhere but the closed eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Midnight returns in a seven-night rhythm. NARRATOR, his gaunt face fixed and his black waistcoat buttoned tight, turns the latch a fraction.

(SOUND: a clock ticks toward twelve; the latch whispers in its socket)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

The door opens by degrees. NARRATOR slips in the closed dark lantern, then slowly thrusts his head through the opening.

(SOUND: the door hinges complain once; NARRATOR freezes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him. And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The same act repeats: lantern closed, head entering, darkness undisturbed.

(SOUND: a faint clock tick stretches into the silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. I undid the lantern just so much that a single thin ray fell toward the vulture eye.

Seven nights pass in fragments. Each time, THE OLD MAN'S eye remains closed. Each dawn, NARRATOR enters openly, smiling.

(SOUND: night wind gives way to morning birds; a bed frame creaks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

At daybreak, NARRATOR stands beside the bed with practiced cheer while THE OLD MAN, unsuspecting, looks up at him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, and spoke courageously to him in a hearty tone, inquiring how he had passed the night. So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The eighth midnight. The door gives no more than a black hairline. NARRATOR holds the closed lantern with both hands, his dark head barely inside the room.

(SOUND: the clock's minute hand ticks; the door wood strains under NARRATOR's touch)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

NARRATOR's mouth twitches with a silent chuckle. THE OLD MAN suddenly shifts upright.

(SOUND: a muffled chuckle; the bed springs snap)

THE OLD MAN
Who's there?

NARRATOR remains perfectly still, his head suspended in the opening.

(SOUND: the house holds its breath; a death-watch ticks inside the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening—just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

A slight tremor passes through THE OLD MAN. His hands clutch the sheet.

(SOUND: a low groan rises from the bed; wind breathes faintly in the chimney)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

NARRATOR's eye gleams in the darkness.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. He had been saying to himself—“It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor,” or “It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp.”

THE OLD MAN pulls the sheet to his chin, listening to sounds that never explain the darkness.

(SOUND: one tiny scurry across the floor; then silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, he had been trying to comfort himself with these suppositions: but he had found all in vain. All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

The darkness seems to press around NARRATOR's hidden head.

(SOUND: the wall gives a long, hollow creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And it was the mournful influence of the unperceived shadow that caused him to feel—although he neither saw nor heard—to feel the presence of my head within the room.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

After the long silence, the tin cover of the lantern opens by a hair. A single dim ray, thin as a spider's thread, reaches the bed.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge creaks; THE OLD MAN draws one shallow breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When I had waited a long time, very patiently, without hearing him lie down, I resolved to open a little—a very, very little crevice in the lantern. You cannot imagine how stealthily, stealthily, until, at length, a single dim ray shot from out the crevice.

The ray fixes upon THE OLD MAN'S eye. It is wide open.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick beat begins beneath the silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones. I could see nothing else of the old man’s face or person: for I had directed the ray precisely upon the damned spot.

NARRATOR holds the lantern motionless, scarcely breathing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense? Now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart.

The beat quickens. NARRATOR's hand trembles but keeps the ray on the eye.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows quicker and louder, like a drum beneath the floor)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But even yet I refrained and kept still. I scarcely breathed. I held the lantern motionless. I tried how steadily I could maintain the ray upon the eye.

NARRATOR's face tightens. The shutters seem to shudder with the force of the sound.

(SOUND: heartbeat pounds through the wall; the distant house remains dead quiet)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant. The old man’s terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!

NARRATOR glances toward the walls, terrified that someone outside will hear.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man’s hour had come!

NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the room.

(SOUND: the lantern snaps open; THE OLD MAN shrieks once—once only; the bed crashes over him)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done.

Under the heavy bed, the muffled heart continues to beat.

(SOUND: a dull heartbeat throbs beneath the bed, then grows faint)

NARRATOR presses both hands against the bed. At length, the sound ceases.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

NARRATOR drags the bed away and examines the corpse. He places his hand over the heart and waits.

(SOUND: cloth rustles beneath his palm; then absolute silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I removed the bed and examined the corpse. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - PRE-DAWN

The night wanes. NARRATOR, sleeves rolled over his black waistcoat, works beside a tub and three lifted floorboards.

(SOUND: a knife is set down; wood groans under the lifted planks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

NARRATOR works with controlled precision.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

The three planks lie open. NARRATOR lowers the remains between the scantlings.

(SOUND: floorboards scrape; the house gives a low settling sigh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings.

He replaces the boards, fitting each one into place until the floor is smooth again.

(SOUND: three boards knock softly into their grooves)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

INT. OLD HOUSE - ENTRY HALL - PRE-DAWN

The entry hall is black. NARRATOR, pale and disordered but still wearing the black waistcoat, crosses to the street door.

(SOUND: a clock strikes four; a hard knocking follows at the door)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o’clock—still dark as midnight. As the bell sounded the hour, there came a knocking at the street door.

NARRATOR opens the door. OFFICER HALE, OFFICER MOSS, and OFFICER REED enter in damp nineteenth-century police coats, their manner perfectly composed.

(SOUND: boots cross the threshold; the door shuts against the wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night; suspicion of foul play had been aroused; information had been lodged at the police office, and they had been deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR smiles and bows them inside.

NARRATOR
The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

INT. OLD HOUSE - OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - PRE-DAWN

NARRATOR, gaunt and pale beneath his close dark hair, leads the three officers into THE OLD MAN'S chamber. The sealed shutters and undisturbed treasures surround the smooth floorboards.

(SOUND: boots climb the stairs; floorboards creak under the officers' weight)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I took my visitors all over the house. I bade them search—search well. I led them, at length, to his chamber. I showed them his treasures, secure, undisturbed.

The officers inspect the room. NARRATOR brings in chairs and places his own directly above the hidden corpse.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape across the boards)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room, and desired them here to rest from their fatigues, while I myself, in the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

OFFICER HALE, OFFICER MOSS, and OFFICER REED sit. Their lips move in easy conversation. NARRATOR answers with a smile.

(SOUND: familiar chatter, chair creaks, wind faintly rattles the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

NARRATOR's color drains. One hand presses against his temple.

(SOUND: a thin ringing begins inside the room)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

The officers continue to chat pleasantly. NARRATOR speaks faster, gesturing to keep their attention.

(SOUND: the ringing grows more distinct beneath the officers' voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling: but it continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

NARRATOR looks down at the boards beneath his chair.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick beat begins under the floor)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. No doubt I now grew very pale; but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice. Yet the sound increased—and what could I do?

NARRATOR rises and argues with empty gestures while the officers remain at ease.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows louder; the officers' mild chatter continues)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I arose and argued about trifles, in a high key and with violent gesticulations; but the noise steadily increased. I paced the floor to and fro with heavy strides, as if excited to fury by the observations of the men.

NARRATOR seizes his chair and grates it across the boards.

(SOUND: chair legs shriek against the floor, but the heartbeat rises over all)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased.

OFFICER HALE, OFFICER MOSS, and OFFICER REED continue to smile and chat. NARRATOR foams, raves, and clutches at his coat.

(SOUND: heartbeat—louder, louder, louder—fills the old house)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

NARRATOR grips the edge of the table, eyes fixed on the three floorboards.

(SOUND: the heartbeat pounds with unbearable force; the officers' chatter falls thin beneath it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But anything was better than this agony! Anything was more tolerable than this derision! I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die! and now—again!—hark! louder! louder! louder! louder!

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The three officers stare at the floorboards. NARRATOR's hands fall still.

(SOUND: the heartbeat cuts off, leaving only the dead silence of the old house)

FADE OUT.

THE END
